class Solution {
  public int[] sortingPressProofStrip(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {class Sorter {int[]sort(int[]xs){if(xs.length<2)return xs;int m=xs.length/2;int[]left=sort(Arrays.copyOfRange(xs,0,m)),right=sort(Arrays.copyOfRange(xs,m,xs.length)),merged=new int[xs.length];int i=0,j=0,t=0;while(i<left.length||j<right.length)merged[t++]=j==right.length||(i<left.length&&left[i]<=right[j])?left[i++]:right[j++];return merged;}}for(int x:new Sorter().sort(a.clone()))out.add(x);}
    return out.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

Sort readings from smallest to largest using merge sort. To use merge sort, split the list into smaller parts, sort those parts, then join the sorted parts in order. Keep all repeated numbers. The result list is the sorted list.

Return the answer

Return the whole result list in the order you built it. If it is empty, return [].

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n log n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

